00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.772
of meeting these challenges is
really becoming significant.

00:02:32.772 --> 00:02:34.230
RANDY SERAFINI:
That's right, Dave.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.180
One emerging example
is the recently

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.800
passed EU General Data
Protection Regulation or GDPR.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.980
DAVE ELLIOTT: GDPR.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.560
That's got to be a
government thing.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.840
RANDY SERAFINI: Yeah, say
that five times really fast.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.750
This new regulation will go
into effect in Europe in 2018.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.660
It requires a company
to delete data

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.890
if it is no longer used for
the purpose it was collected.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.690
And it requires a company
to delete that data

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:02.690
if an individual revokes
the consent for that company

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.160
to hold the data.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.070
Regulations like these have
far reaching implications

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:11.142
to the storage data protection
backup and archiving teams.

00:03:11.142 --> 00:03:12.350
DAVE ELLIOTT: Got it, got it.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:15.640
So it's data growth, fixed
budgets, business alignment,

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.170
and then the
security compliance,

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.480
these are things that are
significant challenges now.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:19.910
RANDY SERAFINI: That's correct.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:20.495
DAVE ELLIOTT: In backup.

00:03:20.495 --> 00:03:20.970
RANDY SERAFINI: Yep.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.845
DAVE ELLIOTT: OK, so
let's think a little bit

00:03:22.845 --> 00:03:24.470
about some of the
challenges then

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.670
of existing backup targets.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.030
When you think about cloud,
you think about, well,

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.380
what's the alternative?

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.400
So most of you probably
use or have used tape

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.290
as your primary backup target.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.330
Tape, however, has
many, many limitations

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.680
which are significant and
are becoming more and more

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.640
problematic in a world where
data security compliance

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.390
and availability are critical.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.300
First, let's talk about
the speed of tape.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.082
No matter how
modern the library,

00:03:47.082 --> 00:03:48.790
you're still working
from the limitations

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:50.250
of a linear technology.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.520
Plus tape is usually
stored off-site,

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.940
and it's, of course,
offline, meaning

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.690
it could take hours
or even days for you

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:59.040
to get the tape back on premise.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.400
While nothing's new about
this problem in tape,

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.260
it's more significant of
a limitation in a world

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.420
where on demand, always
on is status quo.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.930
Second, remember that
tape is a manual process

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.950
with physical devices tapes,
autoloaders, libraries,

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.170
that are managed by humans.

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:20.550
In economic terms, tape
can be labor intensive,

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.130
but in IT terms
it's just a pain.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.690
That brings me to the third
major challenge of tape.

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.910
It can be an unreliable process.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.230
It's error prone,
not necessarily

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:32.540
because tape is unreliable,
but because there

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:36.830
are multiple steps, multiple
devices, and multiple people.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.834
And that leads to multiple
potential points of failure.

00:04:40.834 --> 00:04:43.500
RANDY SERAFINI: Dave I think you
made a mistake with your slide.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.237
Where's the icon
for error prone?

00:04:45.237 --> 00:04:46.820
DAVE ELLIOTT: Well,
I had a great one,

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.840
but I accidentally fat
fingered it and deleted it.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:50.924
You know, I am only human.

00:04:50.924 --> 00:04:52.339
RANDY SERAFINI:
Well, now you know

00:04:52.339 --> 00:04:53.970
that's why people need backup.

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.095
DAVE ELLIOTT: That's right.

00:04:55.095 --> 00:04:58.990
You need to have a secondary
copy and not a manual and error

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.630
prone one like tape management.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:05.005
So let's move on to the
other popular target disk.

00:05:05.005 --> 00:05:07.410
Disk is ideal for
delivery of hot data

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.700
and has a lot of uses in backup
such as for deduplication.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.750
However, a lot of
folks overuse disk.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.930
It's not designed for long
term retention, it's expensive,

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.010
and depending on your
architecture and design

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:21.884
can be really, really
difficult to scale.

00:05:21.884 --> 00:05:24.550
So now, let's move on to some of
the key considerations of cloud

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:28.140
storage as you think about
using it for cloud backup.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.480
So we'd like to
touch on three areas

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.340
technical considerations,
financial considerations,

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.660
and operational considerations.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.410
Let's start with
technical considerations.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.285
This is the one that
most people think about.

00:05:39.285 --> 00:05:40.780
First, storage has
to be reliable.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.150
You simply can't ever lose data.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:46.600
This might be represented
as durability, redundancy,

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.710
or geographic diversity.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.297
Second, storage
should be available.

00:05:51.297 --> 00:05:52.630
This is more and more important.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:56.830
Can I get my data when
and where I need it?

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:00.470
This might be reflected in
latency, global reach, network

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:04.130
performance, network
performance and stability,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.460
and will likely have some
sort of service level

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:07.800
agreement attached to it.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.482
Third, the data has to
be safe in the cloud.

00:06:10.482 --> 00:06:11.940
As you mentioned
earlier, you can't

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.300
afford to fail in this front.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:16.840
Important considerations
is the type and location

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:20.070
of encryption, access
control mechanisms,

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.400
and a track record, a track
record of safety and security.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.880
Ask your cloud vendor.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.670
Next, let's look at
financial considerations.

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.470
Prices continue
to fall in cloud,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.730
so let's take a look at how the
tape, disk, and cloud generally

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:35.870
compare.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:39.420
So capital outlay or hardware
purchasing, disk is expensive

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.250
and cloud requires
no upfront purchase.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.600
So cloud has an advantage there.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.430
Capacity costs or ongoing
cost are typically

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:50.540
very low for tape, but remember
that's only a part of storage.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.720
For cloud, in
comparison to tape,

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.210
you only pay for what you
actually use and you use it

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:57.500
when you actually need it.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:02.660
While tape has shipping and
logistics for off-site storage.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.430
Networking cost is
higher for cloud

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.560
as you import and export data.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.850
Personnel costs are higher
for tape as it requires,

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:12.940
as you mentioned, some
human intervention,

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.960
some human activity, and
has higher recovery costs.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.210
So to find out if it makes
economic sense for you,

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:21.260
I recommend putting
together a spreadsheet that

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.700
takes into account
the real cost of tape

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.340
and the real cost of cloud.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.330
We'll be doing another
in-depth webinar

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:30.690
on the ROI of tape versus cloud,
so keep an eye out for that.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.940
So now, let's take a look
at the third major component

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:35.410
here the operational
considerations of the cloud.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:38.470
So moving to the cloud you may
have organizational resistance

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:39.660
from vested interest.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:42.480
It may be somebody's job it
is to manage the backups,

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:43.880
and they don't want to change.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.070
Change is hard.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:46.890
You may have resistance
from security teams

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:50.374
or from traditional IT
that's not yet sold on cloud.

00:07:50.374 --> 00:07:52.040
So generally a best
practice to overcome

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:54.980
that organizational
resistance is get buy in early

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:56.740
and make them a
part of the process.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.890
Engage your vendors engage
us to help you alleviate

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:00.930
some of these concerns.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:01.765
Second is workflow.

00:08:01.765 --> 00:08:03.600
So some of the
organizational resistance

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.830
might come from
people's jobs changing.

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:07.770
So how do you best optimize?

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:11.250
Generally speaking, I would say
think about it ahead of time.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:16.060
Prepare for having those
individuals be reassigned

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:20.040
to value added services
to create better alignment

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:21.620
with the business.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.530
You also are going to have
vested interest in existing

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:26.792
vendors for storage and backup.

00:08:26.792 --> 00:08:28.500
And generally speaking,
the best practice

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:30.409
there is simply be
prepared to switch

00:08:30.409 --> 00:08:31.950
though that isn't
typically something

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:33.390
that is a major inhibitor.

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:34.990
Finally, integration.

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:37.760
How difficult is it to move
to the crowd, how tightly

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.742
integrated is a solution?

00:08:39.742 --> 00:08:41.200
Your backup software
should make it

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:45.600
easy to use disk,
tape, or cloud.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.450
Great, so now let's
talk about NetBackup

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:49.650
and how NetBackup
makes it really,

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.200
really simple to
move to the cloud

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:55.790
and really if you can start with
just explaining how NetBackup

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:57.380
views the world
and how NetBackup

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:58.960
is a differentiated product.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.251
RANDY SERAFINI: Sure, I'd be
happy to, Dave, thank you.

00:09:01.251 --> 00:09:02.810
When we talk about
NetBackup we are

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:05.780
