00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.840
For nearly as long
as I can remember,

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.560
and I've been doing
this for a while,

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.460
the typical enterprise is
a complex, heterogeneous

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:16.210
environment with a wide
range of applications running

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.940
in virtual systems,
physical systems,

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:20.960
and are globally
deployed in multiple data

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.460
centers with potentially
thousands of remote offices

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:25.500
everywhere.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.290
When you layer
massive data growth

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:29.760
on top of these complex
global deployments

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:33.060
coming from potentially
hundreds of thousands of VMs

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.000
as we see in some of our
largest enterprise customers

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.020
on the planet, you need to
be able to scale and manage

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:39.737
petabytes.

00:09:39.737 --> 00:09:40.820
DAVE ELLIOTT: Or exabytes.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.290
RANDY SERAFINI: Or
exabytes of data

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.790
no matter where it is located
without loss in performance.

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:48.000
What enterprise
customers tell us is they

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.760
don't want to run multiple
point products for their data

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.030
protection.

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:55.110
The reason for this is they
end up with siloed solutions,

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:58.250
what not only adds cost
and are hard to scale

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:00.630
but introduces significant risk.

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:03.840
This additional cost to
train, manage, and orchestrate

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.170
multiple products, but because
the data protection is now

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.770
fragmented, significant
risk is introduced

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:11.890
through gaps in
their data protection

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:15.120
that might be discovered
only when it's too late.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.940
Imagine the challenges of
trying to orchestrate recovery

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.112
from multiple products in the
event of a major disaster.

00:10:22.112 --> 00:10:23.570
This is where I'd
