These are cloud services that
you can apply to your data

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:10.580
when it's in our cloud.

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:13.980
Things like data analytics and
machine learning for instance.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.440
Fifth, all of Google's
cloud storage offerings

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:19.190
use a consistent single API.

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:21.760
No need to write API
for standard storage

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:23.000
and another for archive.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:25.310
And then finally,
the tight integration

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:27.880
with backup solutions such
as NetBackup and Backup Exec,

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:31.320
means that it's very
simple to get started.

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:33.650
So now, I want to briefly
introduce Google Cloud

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:35.370
Storage's services.

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:37.960
So we have three
specific services.

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:39.800
The first is standard
storage, which

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:42.300
is highly performant
and highly available

00:21:42.300 --> 00:21:44.790
and is ideally suited
for hot applications.

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:48.380
Durable, reduced availability
is exactly what its name says,

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:50.810
it provides slightly
lower availability

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:52.740
with the same durability
and performance

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:54.900
and it costs a little bit less.

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:57.710
Nearline, though, is
ideally designed for backup.

00:21:57.710 --> 00:22:01.980
Nearline is what NetBackup has
tightly integrated into its UI.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:06.060
It's low cost, only a
penny a gig per month.

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:09.370
But you do pay for
reads, so it's ideally

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:12.810
designed for those
use cases where

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:16.560
you don't read the data
frequently such as backup.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:19.690
It's highly durable 11.9,
durability built in.

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:23.680
And again, it's integrated
with NetBackup and Backup Exec.

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:26.450
So now, let's close with
a few ideas on where

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:28.070
you can go from here.

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:32.160
What and when do you
move data to the cloud?

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:35.500
Data moving to the cloud, any
data that's older than 30 days

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:39.660
I think 90% of backup
restores are from data

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:41.060
that's less than 30 days.

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:42.894
So after 30 days you
can move it to a cloud.

00:22:42.894 --> 00:22:44.184
RANDY SERAFINI: That's correct.

00:22:44.184 --> 00:22:46.270
But there's another
interesting statistic, Dave.

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:48.770
And that is research
has shown that

00:22:48.770 --> 00:22:52.900
after 90 days, 97% of the data--

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:54.050
DAVE ELLIOTT: 97%, OK.

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:56.727
RANDY SERAFINI: 97%
is no longer accessed.

00:22:56.727 --> 00:22:57.560
So think about that.

00:22:57.560 --> 00:22:59.750
You have all this data
sitting on expensive disk

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:01.610
after 90 days just
sitting there.

00:23:01.610 --> 00:23:02.750
DAVE ELLIOTT: So move it.

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:04.130
RANDY SERAFINI: What's a
good thing to do with it?

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:06.463
DAVE ELLIOTT: Move it to the
cloud as quickly as you can

00:23:06.463 --> 00:23:08.010
after 30 days or 60 days.

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:12.230
So data and remote office and
branches, the typical robo use

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:14.000
case is a great place
for you, the type

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.000
of data to move to the
cloud because you already

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:17.500
have to traverse the network.

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:20.310
A secondary copy of
backup data so disk

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:23.050
to disk to cloud as
you might call it

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:25.160
is a great type
of data that gets

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:29.080
moved, data that's generally
on prem that has filled up

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:32.520
